Fixes: 2d6ad41b2c21 ("powerpc/powernv: use the generic iommu bypass code")
Signed-off-by: Alexey Kardashevskiy <aik@ozlabs.ru>
Signed-off-by: Michael Ellerman <mpe@ellerman.id.au>

Here is the root cause:
The root cause of the bug was the incorrect handling of return values in the function `pnv_pci_ioda_iommu_bypass_supported`, where the return type was changed from `int` to `bool`. This led to instances where error codes (like `-ENODEV`) were still being returned instead of boolean values, which could cause unexpected behavior when the function was called, particularly in scenarios where bypass support was not available.

Here is the patch:

 static bool pnv_pci_ioda_iommu_bypass_supported(struct pci_dev *pdev,
 		u64 dma_mask)
 {
 	struct pci_controller *hose = pci_bus_to_host(pdev->bus);
 	struct pnv_phb *phb = hose->private_data;
 	struct pci_dn *pdn = pci_get_pdn(pdev);
 	struct pnv_ioda_pe *pe;

 	if (WARN_ON(!pdn || pdn->pe_number == IODA_INVALID_PE))
-		return -ENODEV;
+		return false;

 	pe = &phb->ioda.pe_array[pdn->pe_number];
 	if (pe->tce_bypass_enabled) {
 		u64 top = pe->tce_bypass_base + memblock_end_of_DRAM() - 1;
 		if (dma_mask >= top)
 			return true;
 	}

 	/*
 	 * If the device can't set the TCE bypass bit but still wants
 	 * to access 4GB or more, on PHB3 we can reconfigure TVE#0 to
 	 * bypass the 32-bit region and be usable for 64-bit DMAs.
 	 * The device needs to be able to address all of this space.
 	 */
 	if (dma_mask >> 32 &&
 	    dma_mask > (memory_hotplug_max() + (1ULL << 32)) &&
 	    /* pe->pdev should be set if it's a single device, pe->pbus if not */
 	    (pe->device_count == 1 || !pe->pbus) &&
 	    phb->model == PNV_PHB_MODEL_PHB3) {
 		/* Configure the bypass mode */
 		s64 rc = pnv_pci_ioda_dma_64bit_bypass(pe);
 		if (rc)
-			return rc;
+			return false;
 		/* 4GB offset bypasses 32-bit space */
 		pdev->dev.archdata.dma_offset = (1ULL << 32);
 		return true;
 	}

 	return false;
 }



